WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.633
The LLM as judge pattern.

2
00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:05.907
Humans can't grade thousands of outputs a day.

3
00:00:05.907 --> 00:00:08.233
A model can, if you know its biases.

4
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:13.633
String matching can't grade open ended text, and humans don't scale.

5
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:20.467
So a strong model receives a written rubric, with criteria in priority order and a fixed output format.

6
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:22.333
Plus the outputs to grade.

7
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.633
The judge is the model in the middle.

8
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:27.530
It returns a score or a pairwise verdict.

9
00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.000
This is how RAGAS computes its metrics.

10
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.067
Trust a judge only after you know its biases.

11
00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:38.091
Position bias: judges favor whichever answer comes first.

12
00:00:38.091 --> 00:00:40.967
Run every pair twice with the order swapped.

13
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:44.031
Verbosity bias: longer answers score higher.

14
00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:46.733
Put brevity in the rubric, and cap lengths.

15
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:50.293
Self preference: models like their own family's style.

16
00:00:50.293 --> 00:00:52.467
Judge with a different model family.

17
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.033
Here is the swap test.

18
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.933
We judge the pair twice, once with each order.

19
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:01.667
Then translate each verdict back to answer a or answer b.

20
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:04.372
If both rounds agree, that's a real win.

21
00:01:04.372 --> 00:01:07.800
If they flip, it was position bias, and we call it a tie.

22
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.267
Now compare two reranker configs on fifty questions.

23
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.900
Without a swap, config B wins thirty four to sixteen.

24
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.872
With the swap test, eleven verdicts flip and become ties.

25
00:01:19.872 --> 00:01:23.094
The honest tally is twenty five, fourteen, eleven.

26
00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:25.800
Still a win for B, but a much smaller one.

27
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:33.467
The team also noticed that a same family judge rated its own house style a suspicious point four higher.

28
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.133
The fix: swap the order, and use a different model family.

29
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:39.124
Let's recap.

30
00:01:39.124 --> 00:01:41.167
A judge needs a written rubric.

31
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.800
Fight position bias by swapping order.

32
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.800
Fight verbosity bias by rewarding brevity.

33
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.437
And fight self preference with another model family.

34
00:01:50.437 --> 00:01:52.233
Next up: benchmark creation.
